I&#x27;m not sure that&#x27;s the case. I haven&#x27;t seen even the smaller US models get stuck in a thinking loop, while with DS models I often get thinking traces that look like<p><pre><code>  Now foo.cpp:
  &lt;1000 lines&gt;
  Wait, no.
  &lt;the same 1000 lines with 1 change somewhere in line 359&gt;
  Let me reconsider.
  &lt;the same fucking 1000 lines&gt;
  OK, finalizing.
  &lt;the same 1000 lines&gt;
  Now write the file:
  &lt;&#x2F;thinking&gt;
  &lt;TOOL CALL: write the 1000 lines&gt;</code></pre>

You can just set reasoning_effort: low.<p>Also, I don&#x27;t know why you&#x27;re singling out Chinese models here. Look at Fable 5.2:
&quot;The output tokens per task just exploded. From 118k tokens (46k Answer&#x2F;71k Reasoning) in 5.1 to 274k tokens (81k Answer&#x2F;192k Reasoning) in 5.2.&quot;
<a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50432202">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=50432202</a><p>I suspect this comes down to the so-called anti-distillation changes, where they dropped retained reasoning so you can&#x27;t see what the models are actually thinking. I say &quot;so-called&quot; because these changes conveniently showed up right after that paper came out on Opus 4.8&#x27;s decoded reasoning, which proved the models were cheating on the benchmark questions they tested.
